Data-flow patterns that turned out to be right in more than one place. Proven
useful, therefore worth reaching for first the next time something looks similar.

--

## Choose the structure so the invariant cannot be broken.

Blocks could have been polygons. They are faces of a network instead, and the
reason is that adjacency then costs nothing and cannot silently rot: two blocks
are neighbours because they literally name the same edge record, not because
somebody measured that they were close.

The polygon version has a failure mode where dragging a shared corner tears the
fence, the two blocks stop being adjacent, and everything still looks correct on
screen. The network version cannot express that state at all.

**When a property matters, look for the representation in which violating it is
unrepresentable** — rather than the one where you check for violations afterward.
The check you write is a check you can forget to run.

## Absence is a value, and usually the most informative one.

A filter answers each block with a number, or with nothing. Nothing is not zero.
Zero means the fire risk here is low; nothing means you have no idea.

That one allowance deleted a whole subsystem. There is no fog of war to build, no
confidence channel, no greyed-out unknown state — ignorance draws as bare
painting, which is both correct and beautiful. **Before adding a channel to say
"we don't know," check whether the existing channel can simply decline to
answer.**

## Refusing a channel dissolves a class of problems.

The map carries no text. That was chosen because the painting is too visually
dense for a typeface to sit on — and it also meant label collision, a genuinely
hard problem that produces flickering mediocre results, never has to be solved.

The same shape appeared three more times: no distances means no perspective
correction; one texture means no tile pyramid; time is only ever now means no
"this isn't real" indicator. **A capability you decline is a category of bug you
will never fix.** Count the problems a restriction deletes before deciding it is
a sacrifice.

## Weave, do not stack.

Two hatchings drawn on top of each other bury one of them. Two hatchings whose
crossings alternate — decided by the parity of the line indices, the way real
basket weave works — leave both readable, and the trick keeps working at three
and four sets where stacking has long since gone to mud.

**Where several things must occupy one space, look for the rule that shares the
overlaps evenly instead of the rule that orders them.** Ordering scales to about
two.

## A value that two unrelated systems read belongs to neither.

The hour started as a parameter of the shade filter. Then it turned out that
where every person is, is also a function of the hour. Two independent readers
means it is not the filter's — it is global, and sits above all of them.

The tell is worth memorising: **the moment a second, unrelated consumer appears
for a value, its home is wrong.** It was not wrong when it was written; it became
wrong.

## Generate, then view. Never both at once.

The tracing tool writes the fence network; the game only reads it. A filter's
reading is computed separately from the hatching that draws it, and should be
checkable with nothing on screen.

Every time this split exists, a bug has a side and the search space halves. Every
time it does not, a wrong pixel and a wrong rule look identical.
